    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d221">

                <pb facs="0329" n="231" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg231"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.1132/26: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-11-29T14:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 29,
                                1920—2 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 8 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>258. My telegram no. 256 of today.<anchor n="88" xml:id="d221-f1"/> After a
                    consultation with Mr. Wadsworth I cannot ascertain that an official invitation
                    has been extended to the Secretary of State to visit Argentina, or that he will
                    be entertained as a national guest. I learn from an unimpeachable <pb
                        facs="0330" n="232" xml:id="pg232"/> private source that several weeks ago
                    the Argentine Ambassador at Washington cabled his Government to send a torpedo
                    boat to bring Mr. Colby and his party to Buenos Aires from Montevideo, but that
                    this request was negatived by President Irigoyen.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
